<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>FreePolarAlign.Devices.Ascom</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>FreePolarAlign.Devices.Ascom</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Allows this Windows-targeted project to be *compiled* (not run) from the
      full solution on macOS/Linux dev machines. It is deliberately excluded
      from FreePolarAlign.Core.slnf, the filter used for day-to-day macOS work.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Compiled against, never shipped beside.

    A plugin loads in an isolated context (D4), and PluginAssemblyLoadContext
    resolves an assembly from the plugin's own directory before falling back to
    the host. So a copy of FreePolarAlign.Core or FreePolarAlign.Imaging sitting
    next to this dll is loaded as a *second*, distinct assembly identity, with
    the host's copy still loaded alongside it. Measured, by pointing that load
    context at a folder shaped like the published one: with the copies present
    the plugin's Imaging and the host's are different assemblies; with them
    absent the resolver returns nothing, the side-by-side probe finds nothing,
    and the host's is used.

    The load context already hard-codes an exemption for FreePolarAlign.Devices,
    the contract assembly, precisely because a duplicate of it would make every
    provider fail `is IDeviceProvider` against a type that merely looks the
    same. Core and Imaging get no such exemption, so the only safe answer is not
    to ship them.

    The version skew is the worse half in practice. A plugin folder republished
    one build later than the host keeps an *older* Core and Imaging and runs
    against those, while the session log reports only the host's stamp, so the
    binary being diagnosed would not be the binary running. That is the failure
    CLAUDE.md's versioning rule exists to stop.

    Private=false keeps them out of the build output; ExcludeAssets=runtime
    keeps them out of publish and out of the deps.json runtime list.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FreePolarAlign.Devices\FreePolarAlign.Devices.csproj"
                      Private="false" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
    <!-- For ApparentPlace: a driver configured for JNow needs its coordinates converted
         to and from the J2000 the IMount contract speaks; see AscomMount's doc comment. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FreePolarAlign.Core\FreePolarAlign.Core.csproj"
                      Private="false" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
    <!-- Captured images are written as FITS with the project's own writer (Phase 0), not a
         new format-specific implementation; see AscomCamera.ExposeAsync. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FreePolarAlign.Imaging\FreePolarAlign.Imaging.csproj"
                      Private="false" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
